# Presence, people and shared-screen privacy

A tracker reports on a device or an integration. It does not tell you for certain where a person is. Show only the personal detail the task needs, especially on a wall display or in a screenshot.

**HA-CTRL-014: reference design specification.** This page specifies the design coverage requested by HA-GAP-014 for an existing Home Assistant capability. The examples are local previews with invented values. They install no adapter, connect to no backend and operate no equipment.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-014-capabilities">Task and capability contract</h2>

| Information | Contract |
| --- | --- |
| Person entity | Use the person's name and the native combined tracker state. Do not rebuild the order of trackers in theme code. |
| User account | Keep sign-in and permissions apart from tracked presence. |
| Source and time | Say what reported the state and when, where Home Assistant provides it. Do not call an old report current. |
| Zone or coordinates | Show only the detail the task and the audience justify. A zone name can itself be sensitive. |
| Avatar | Use an approved image or a neutral fallback. Do not infer identity, role or relationship from how someone looks. |

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-014-states">States, pending work and failure</h2>

The Person integration sets a person's state from their connected device trackers. It uses the most recently updated connected connection tracker first, then the most recently updated position tracker, and otherwise the latest tracker that reports `not_home`. The state is `home`, `not_home` or the name of a zone, shown as Home, Away or the zone name. The person also carries the latitude, longitude, `gps_accuracy` and `in_zones` of the tracker that supplied the state. Keep that result and its source. Do not pick whichever tracker gives the most reassuring answer.

Unknown or unavailable tracker information is not Away. A phone left at home, a delayed update or two trackers that disagree can all make presence uncertain. If a current report is missing, you may show an earlier one, but only with its time and a note that it is not current.

One sensor cannot show that the household is empty or that a person is safe. Do not infer that a device is offline, that someone is absent or that a health event has happened without evidence for it.

Say who a privacy choice is for. A public screenshot, a shared wall display and a signed-in private view have different audiences. Leaving personal text off a card does not remove the data from Home Assistant or take away anyone's native access. If a redaction you chose cannot be done, say so rather than showing the detail anyway.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-014-content">Content and interpretation</h2>

Use the native state: Home, Away or the zone name. Where certainty matters, add the source and time next to it, for example “Reported home”. Keep the names people typed exactly. Do not write “Everyone is away” from a partial set of trackers. Explain why a location is needed before asking for it. In public examples, use invented names and leave out real coordinates, routes and faces.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-014-accessibility">Accessibility and interaction</h2>

Show a text name and the reported state. Do not use an avatar or a coloured dot alone. An avatar that repeats a nearby name can be decorative. A control that is only an image of a person needs an accessible name. Do not announce every location update to the whole room. Keep timestamps readable and keyboard details available, and give a text alternative to any map information that is essential. See [Maps, zones and location records](maps-and-zones.html).

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-014-integration">Native integration boundary</h2>

Keep the native Person, tracker and Map behaviour, including the order in which trackers are used and account permissions. A person does not need a sign-in. One without a login can still be tracked, appear on a map and be used in automations. The theme does not provide consent management, location redaction or detailed access rules. These examples are wording and disclosure patterns. A future shared-display feature needs a stated scope, the least data it can show, and tests of the other routes to the same data as well as the main card.

